/**
@page extra_docs Extra Documentation and Design Notes

@section ext_t1 Graph Representation and Parsing

@subsection ext_t1_data Data model

The implementation separates the problem into four layers:

- @ref ProgramPoint "ProgramPoint": one program line plus an optional `+` / `-` marker.
- @ref LiveRange "LiveRange": one input line from the ranges file.
- @ref Web "Web": a merged set of live ranges for the same variable.
- `Graph<int>`: the interference graph, where each vertex stores a web id.

The provided graph structure from the course was preserved as the primary graph
representation. The only conceptual adaptation is that vertex labels are `int`
web identifiers instead of domain objects. This keeps the graph reusable and
lets the allocation algorithms work with a compact vertex set.

@subsection ext_t1_webs Web construction

The input file may contain multiple live ranges for the same variable. Those
ranges are merged into a web whenever they share at least one program point.
The implementation uses a union-find structure over the live ranges of a single
variable:

- Build one union-find element per live range.
- For every pair of ranges of the same variable, test whether they overlap.
- Union overlapping pairs.
- Emit one web per connected component.

Two implementation details matter for correctness and testing:

- The emitted webs are sorted deterministically by variable name and first live
  line.
- The final web ids are reassigned after sorting so output files are stable.

@subsection ext_t1_interference Interference rule

Two webs interfere if they are simultaneously live at some program point.
However, the project statement introduces an important exception:

- if one web ends on line `L` due to a last use (`-`) and another web starts on
  the same line `L` due to a definition (`+`), they do **not** interfere.

This rule is implemented explicitly in
@ref InterferenceGraph::interferes "InterferenceGraph::interferes".

@subsection ext_t1_output Aggregated web output

The output format uses **one line per web**, not one line per constituent live
range. Internally, this means:

- collect all program points from all ranges of the web;
- sort them by line number;
- collapse duplicate line numbers;
- if the same line contains both `+` and `-`, treat it as an internal merge
  point and print it without a marker.

That matches the examples from the project statement, especially the fused-web
examples.

@subsection ext_t1_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Parse ranges | `O(L * P)` |
| Parse config | `O(L)` |
| Build webs | `O(V * R^2 * P)` |
| Build interference graph | `O(W^2 * P)` |

---

@section ext_t2 T2.1 - Basic Register Allocation

@subsection ext_t2_basic_approach Approach

The basic allocator follows the simplify/select structure from the statement:

- Maintain the set of active vertices.
- Repeatedly remove every active vertex with degree `< K` and push it onto a
  stack.
- If no such vertex exists, pick a spill candidate heuristically and remove it.
- Pop the stack and greedily assign the first available register.

In the **basic** mode, any spill means the allocation is considered infeasible.
The service layer then converts the result into the specification-compliant
fallback output:

- `registers: 0`
- every web mapped to `M`

@subsection ext_t2_basic_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Simplification + select | `O(W * (W + E))` |

---

@section ext_t3 T2.2 - Register Allocation with Spilling

@subsection ext_t3_spill_approach Approach

For the spilling mode, spilling is no longer treated as a failure. The algorithm:

- tries to color the graph with zero forced spills;
- if that fails, forces one spill and retries;
- continues until either a valid coloring is found or the configured bound `K`
  is exhausted.

The spill heuristic is intentionally conservative:

- prefer the currently highest-degree web;
- break ties by lower web id to keep results deterministic.

When a valid coloring is found, only the selected spilled webs are mapped to
memory. Unlike the basic mode, the result remains feasible.

@subsection ext_t3_spill_example Example

The triangle below needs 3 colors, but with `K = 2` registers it becomes
allocatable after spilling one web:

<div class="interactive_dotgraph">

\dotfile dox/example_spilling.dot

</div>

@subsection ext_t3_spill_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Up to `K` recoloring attempts | `O(K * W * (W + E))` |

---

@section ext_t4 T2.3 - Register Allocation with Splitting

@subsection ext_t4_split_approach Approach

Web splitting is used when the user explicitly selects `splitting, K`.
The implementation keeps the problem local and deterministic:

- first, try to color the current graph with no splitting;
- if that fails, pick one of the currently uncolorable/high-degree webs;
- split it into two derived webs;
- rebuild the interference graph;
- retry coloring;
- stop after at most `K` successful split operations.

Two split strategies are used:

- if the original web already contains multiple ranges, split those ranges into
  two ordered groups;
- if it contains only one range, split the sequence of program points into two
  contiguous derived ranges and normalize their endpoints with `+` and `-`.

This heuristic is not globally optimal, but it is simple, deterministic, and
captures the intended idea of reducing interference by slicing a long-lived web.

@subsection ext_t4_split_example Example

The following case starts as a 3-clique. After splitting `web0` into two derived
webs, the resulting graph becomes 2-colorable:

<div class="interactive_dotgraph">

\dotfile dox/example_splitting.dot

</div>

@subsection ext_t4_split_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Split candidate selection | `O(W + E)` |
| Graph rebuild after each split | `O(W^2 * P)` |
| Full allocator with at most `K` splits | `O(K * (W^2 * P + W * (W + E)))` |

---

@section ext_t5 T2.4 - Custom Allocation Strategy

@subsection ext_t5_free_approach Approach

The custom mode uses a DSATUR-style greedy heuristic:

- repeatedly select the uncolored web with the highest saturation degree;
- break ties using the highest graph degree;
- assign the first compatible register;
- if none exists, spill that web.

This strategy tends to make better local choices than a plain fixed-order greedy
coloring because it colors the most constrained vertices first.

@subsection ext_t5_free_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Saturation-based selection and coloring | `O(W^2 + E)` |

---

@section ext_t6 Error Handling, CLI, and Testing

@subsection ext_t6_cli CLI

The application supports:

- an interactive menu (`./register_alloc`);
- a batch interface required by the statement:
  `./register_alloc -b ranges.txt config.txt allocation.txt`

Batch mode now returns a non-zero exit code when one of the input files cannot be
parsed, which makes the test script dependable.

@subsection ext_t6_tests Testing

The test suite has three goals:

- verify parsing and output formatting corner cases;
- pin down the interference and web-fusion semantics;
- validate the behaviour of all four allocation modes.

The integration tests use the six baseline datasets plus dedicated spilling, splitting, and free scenarios.
*/
